/// Res
///
/// What a written path refers to, recorded ON the node that wrote it.
///
/// A span-keyed side table cannot be exact: two spans that pack to the same
/// key silently overwrite each other, and a synthesized node has no span to
/// key on at all.  A slot on the node has neither failure mode, and it cannot
/// desynchronize from the syntax it answers for.
///
/// Two rules make the answer safe to copy and safe to trust:
///
/// **A `Res` names a definition, never an instantiation.**  `T` stamps
/// `GenericParam(<the id of the declaration that bound T>)`, not whatever `T`
/// is bound to at some call site.
/// Substitution happens strictly below this, in the type layer.  That is what
/// lets `ASTCloner` copy a `Res` verbatim, and it is the difference between
/// this and `NamedAnnotation.pre_resolved`, which holds a `TypeRef` - an
/// instantiation - and therefore has to be dropped on every clone.
///
/// **`Err` is an answer, not a failure.**  It is produced once, with its
/// diagnostic already emitted, and poisons downstream without triggering a
/// second lookup.  There is deliberately no state meaning "did not resolve,
/// try something else": that state is what grows a resolution cascade, because
/// each recovery path added to it becomes a correctness path the next time
/// something reaches it.
///
/// **`Res` has no variant meaning "missing".**  Every variant is an answer, so
/// a consumer holding one has nothing to branch on and no place to put a
/// second strategy.  Absence lives in `ResSlot` below, one type up, where only
/// the resolver can observe it - which is what makes "re-resolve it later"
/// unwriteable rather than merely discouraged.
///
/// `TypeRelative` is a POSITIVE claim of ownership, not a shrug: the language
/// says a path whose first segment names a type is finished by the type layer,
/// which needs a receiver type and so cannot run here.  It must never be used
/// to record that a lookup was attempted and failed - that is `Err` when the
/// name layer is the authority, and a defect in the lookup when it is not.
/// Laundering a tool's limitation into an ownership claim rebuilds the
/// cascade under a better name.
///
/// It carries the base it stopped on and a count of trailing segments, which
/// is what makes that claim checkable rather than merely asserted: a base can
/// only be a type, so a path the name layer failed to walk has nothing to put
/// there and cannot be recorded as type-owned by accident.

namespace compiler::resolver::res;

import std::fmt;

import compiler::resolver::symbol_str;
import compiler::resolver::symbol_str::{ SymbolStr };
import compiler::resolver::symbol_id;
import compiler::resolver::symbol_id::{ SymbolID };

/// The identity of a declaration, as resolution answered it.
///
/// `Res::Def` used to carry the qualified name itself, which made an answer
/// constructible from a spelling: any consumer holding a string could assemble
/// the payload and any consumer holding the payload could re-key a map with
/// it.  A resolution answer that can be rebuilt from the name it answered for
/// is not an answer, it is the name with better manners, and it leaves
/// "re-derive it here" writeable at every use site.
///
/// The field is `private`, so the literal can only be written inside this
/// type's own methods (E0353) and every mint in the tree goes through
/// `of_definition`.  Cryo's visibility is scoped to the declaring TYPE rather
/// than to a module, so this cannot be narrowed further to "only the resolver
/// may mint" - the resolver is as much an outsider to `DefId` as codegen is.
/// What the private field buys is therefore not unforgeability but a single
/// named door: minting and unwrapping are each one greppable call, so the
/// population that turns a name into an answer is countable and can be pinned,
/// which is the same enforcement the lane and b1 ratchets already run.
///
/// It is deliberately NOT a type alias.  An alias would keep every existing
/// string-keyed call compiling, and the point of the distinct type is that the
/// compiler reports the sites which hold only a name - those are not sites
/// needing a different API, they are sites where the resolution answer was
/// discarded and is being recomputed.
type struct DefId {
private:
    /// The canonical qualified name the definition is registered under.
    ///
    /// This stays a `SymbolStr` for now: the maps it keys are `HashMap<u32,
    /// TypeRef>` on `SymbolStr.id`, so a newtype over it is representationally
    /// identical and no consumer's storage changes.  Making it an index into a
    /// definition table is a separate change with its own measurement, because
    /// a type-system change and a data-structure change failing together
    /// cannot be told apart.
    qn: SymbolStr;

public:
    /// The id of the definition registered under `qualified`.
    ///
    /// The one mint.  Every caller is a place that knows the referent for a
    /// reason other than the spelling in front of it - a resolver that just
    /// walked to the declaration, or a synthesizer that built the node and so
    /// holds the type it refers to.  A caller that has only a written name has
    /// not resolved anything and must not reach this.
    static of_definition(qualified: SymbolStr) -> DefId {
        return DefId { qn: qualified };
    }

    /// The id naming no definition, for a slot that must hold one before an
    /// answer exists.  Distinct from `Res::Err`, which is an ANSWER: this is
    /// the absence of one and never reaches a `Res`.
    static invalid() -> DefId {
        return DefId { qn: SymbolStr::empty() };
    }

    /// The canonical name of this definition.
    ///
    /// The one unwrap, and the point at which an answer becomes a string
    /// again.  Legitimate for a diagnostic, a mangled symbol, or any consumer
    /// whose output IS text.  A consumer that unwraps in order to re-key a
    /// lookup has re-derived what it was handed, and should take the `DefId`.
    qualified_name(&this) -> SymbolStr {
        return this.qn;
    }

    /// Whether this names a definition at all.
    is_valid(&this) -> boolean {
        return this.qn.is_valid();
    }

    /// O(1) identity, inherited from interning: two ids naming the same
    /// definition always carry the same handle.
    equals(&this, other: DefId) -> boolean {
        return this.qn.equals(other.qn);
    }
}

/// One signature of one declaration: an entry of the declaration index's
/// function registry, which holds a key, a function type and the linker
/// symbol the definition is emitted under.
///
/// A `DefId` names a declaration; a declaration can carry several
/// signatures (overloads), and two declarations can share a key and a
/// signature and differ only in the symbol (`Display::fmt` and `Debug::fmt`
/// on one type, an inherent `go` beside a trait's `go`, two specializations
/// of one generic method whose signature does not mention its parameter).
/// So neither a key, a `(key, signature)` pair nor a symbol names a
/// definition on its own; the entry does, and it is what a call pins.
///
/// Minted by registration alone: the registrar appends the entry and hands
/// the id back, and stamps it on the declaring node.  A consumer holding
/// one reads the entry's symbol and signature through the index; nothing
/// re-derives either from a spelling.
type struct OverloadId {
private:
    /// Position in the registry's parallel arrays; `0xFFFFFFFF` for none.
    pos: u32;

public:
    /// The id of the entry at registry position `pos`.  Registration's mint.
    static at_position(pos: u32) -> OverloadId {
        return OverloadId { pos: pos };
    }

    /// The id naming no entry, for a slot that must hold one before an
    /// answer exists.
    static invalid() -> OverloadId {
        return OverloadId { pos: 4294967295 };
    }

    /// The registry position.  For the index's own accessors.
    position(&this) -> u32 {
        return this.pos;
    }

    is_valid(&this) -> boolean {
        return this.pos != 4294967295;
    }

    equals(&this, other: OverloadId) -> boolean {
        return this.pos == other.pos;
    }
}

/// The part of a type-relative path the name layer DID finish.
///
/// Exactly the three answers that can name a type, so a base that could not
/// own members - a local, a failure, or another type-relative path - is
/// unrepresentable rather than merely unexpected.  That is what stops
/// `TypeRelative` from degenerating into "something went wrong here": there is
/// no value to put in it unless a type was actually named.
///
/// It is a separate enum because `Res` cannot contain itself by value - an ADT
/// enum is laid out as a tag plus a payload area sized to its largest variant,
/// which for a self-referential variant has no finite size.
type enum ResBase {
    /// A declared type, by the id resolution answered with.
    Def(DefId);
    /// A type parameter, by the symbol its declaration bound - its members
    /// are reached through its bound.  The id and not the spelling, because
    /// two parameters of one spelling in one scope chain are two
    /// declarations, and a stamp carrying the spelling could not say which.
    GenericParam(SymbolID);
    /// A primitive, by its spelling.  Primitives own members without being
    /// declared anywhere, so there is no qualified name to give.
    PrimTy(SymbolStr);
}

implement enum ResBase {
    /// Whether a spelling names a primitive.
    ///
    /// A primitive owns members without being declared anywhere, so it binds in
    /// no scope and appears in no module's export set.  Every lookup therefore
    /// reports it as absent, and only the spelling distinguishes "a primitive,
    /// which needs no declaration" from "a name nothing in the program owns".
    ///
    /// This is the ONE predicate for that question.  The lexer's keyword table
    /// cannot be it: `()` is punctuation, so no keyword spells the unit type,
    /// and a table that cannot express a primitive cannot be the authority on
    /// which spellings are primitive.
    ///
    /// It is deliberately NOT the set of every spelling the lexer accepts as a
    /// type keyword.  `int`, `uint`, `float` and `double` are alias keywords a
    /// module may also be named for - `float` IS one, `std::fmt::float` - and a
    /// scope qualifier answered `PrimTy` here would name the primitive in every
    /// file that does not import the module.  The names below are the ones no
    /// module can also own.
    static is_primitive_spelling(name: string) -> boolean {
        // The unit type owns members without being declared, exactly as the
        // scalars do, and its spelling is likewise its registration key - the
        // declaration index registers `()` beside them.  It reaches here as an
        // impl head's target TEXT; a written `()` in annotation position parses
        // as the empty tuple and never asks this.
        return name.eq("()")
            || name.eq("void")
            || name.eq("boolean")
            || name.eq("char")
            || name.eq("string")
            || name.eq("i8")
            || name.eq("i16")
            || name.eq("i32")
            || name.eq("i64")
            || name.eq("i128")
            || name.eq("u8")
            || name.eq("u16")
            || name.eq("u32")
            || name.eq("u64")
            || name.eq("u128")
            || name.eq("isize")
            || name.eq("usize")
            || name.eq("f32")
            || name.eq("f64")
            || name.eq("never")
            || name.eq("va_list");
    }

    /// The primitive an ALIAS KEYWORD spells, or "" for any other name.
    ///
    /// `int`, `uint`, `float` and `double` are the complement of
    /// `is_primitive_spelling` among the lexer's type keywords: each is
    /// another spelling of a primitive that has its own, and none has an
    /// identity of its own - a `PrimTy` is never recorded under one of these,
    /// only under the spelling it folds to, so one type has one key wherever
    /// a stamp is read.
    ///
    /// A module may also own one of these names (`std::fmt::float`), which is
    /// why the fold is not the answer FIRST: an alias keyword names the
    /// primitive only after every scope has failed to bind it, the way a
    /// prelude alias would.  The name layer asks this at that point and
    /// nowhere earlier; a consumer holding a keyword's own lexeme asks it
    /// too, so the alias table has this one home.
    static primitive_of_alias(name: string) -> string {
        if (name.eq("int"))    { return "i32"; }
        if (name.eq("uint"))   { return "u32"; }
        if (name.eq("float"))  { return "f32"; }
        if (name.eq("double")) { return "f64"; }
        return "";
    }

    to_string(&this) -> string {
        return match (&this) {
            ResBase::Def(_)          => { "Def" }
            ResBase::GenericParam(_) => { "GenericParam" }
            ResBase::PrimTy(_)       => { "PrimTy" }
        };
    }
}

/// A source-location pseudo-constant: a builtin the compiler expands AT THE
/// REFERENCE, to the file or line the reference was written on.
///
/// Like a primitive, it is declared nowhere and so binds in no scope; unlike
/// a primitive it is a VALUE, so it is the answer for a bare identifier that
/// no binding claims and one of these spells.  The kind is the whole answer:
/// a consumer types or expands it by the kind, and the spelling is compared
/// in exactly one place, `of_spelling`.
type enum SourceLoc {
    /// `FILE`: the path of the file the reference is in, a `string`.
    File;
    /// `LINE`: the line the reference is on, a `u32`.
    Line;
}

implement enum SourceLoc {
    /// The pseudo-constant a bare spelling names, or `None` for every other
    /// spelling.  The one place the two names are written.
    static of_spelling(name: string) -> Option<SourceLoc> {
        return match (name) {
            "FILE" => { Option::Some(SourceLoc::File) }
            "LINE" => { Option::Some(SourceLoc::Line) }
            _      => { Option::None }
        };
    }

    to_string(&this) -> string {
        return match (&this) {
            SourceLoc::File => { "FILE" }
            SourceLoc::Line => { "LINE" }
        };
    }
}

type enum Res {
    /// A declaration or module, by the id resolution answered with.
    Def(DefId);
    /// A local binding (parameter or local variable).
    Local(SymbolID);
    /// A type parameter, by the symbol its declaration bound - never the type
    /// it is bound to.
    GenericParam(SymbolID);
    /// A primitive, by its spelling.
    PrimTy(SymbolStr);
    /// A source-location pseudo-constant (`FILE`, `LINE`), by its kind.  An
    /// answer for a bare VALUE name that nothing in scope binds, as `PrimTy`
    /// is for a type spelling; a binding of the same spelling shadows it.
    SourceLoc(SourceLoc);
    /// The type layer owns the REST of this path.  The payload states how far
    /// the name layer got: the type its walk ended on, and how many trailing
    /// segments are left for the type layer to finish.
    ///
    /// Carrying the base is what lets sema resume at a known position instead
    /// of re-searching from a bare name, which is the whole reason a private
    /// resolver grew in sema in the first place.  A marker with no payload
    /// says only "not mine", and "not mine" is not an answer anyone can use.
    TypeRelative(ResBase, u32);
    /// Resolution failed and the diagnostic has already been emitted.
    Err;
}

implement enum Res {
    /// The id of a resolved definition, or an invalid one for every other
    /// answer.  Callers that only accept a definition use this; callers that
    /// must tell one non-definition answer from another match instead.
    ///
    /// This deliberately does not hand back a name.  A consumer that wanted a
    /// name in order to key a lookup is re-deriving what it was already given,
    /// and taking the id is what makes that lookup take the answer instead of
    /// the spelling; one that genuinely emits text asks for the name through
    /// `DefId::qualified_name` and says so at the call site.
    def_id(&this) -> DefId {
        return match (&this) {
            Res::Def(d) => { d }
            _           => { DefId::invalid() }
        };
    }

    /// The symbol a local binding's declaration bound, or an invalid one for
    /// every other answer.  This is the key a pass's own per-binding state
    /// takes: two locals in one function may share a spelling - an inner
    /// block's binding shadows an outer one - and a set keyed by the spelling
    /// gives them one slot, so a move recorded against either is read back
    /// against both.
    local_sym(&this) -> SymbolID {
        return match (&this) {
            Res::Local(s) => { s }
            _             => { SymbolID::invalid() }
        };
    }

    /// The symbol a type parameter's declaration bound, or an invalid one for
    /// every other answer.  This is the key every substitution table takes:
    /// a binding is keyed by the parameter's symbol and never by its
    /// spelling, because a trait default's body is resolved in the trait's
    /// file and copied under an impl whose own parameter may spell the
    /// body's - two declarations the spelling cannot tell apart.
    generic_param_sym(&this) -> SymbolID {
        return match (&this) {
            Res::GenericParam(s) => { s }
            _                    => { SymbolID::invalid() }
        };
    }

    /// The symbol of the type parameter a type-relative path is rooted at
    /// (`I::Item`, `T::default`), or an invalid one when the path is rooted
    /// elsewhere or the answer is not a path at all.
    relative_param_sym(&this) -> SymbolID {
        return match (&this) {
            Res::TypeRelative(b, _) => {
                match (b) {
                    ResBase::GenericParam(s) => { s }
                    _                        => { SymbolID::invalid() }
                }
            }
            _ => { SymbolID::invalid() }
        };
    }

    to_string(&this) -> string {
        return match (&this) {
            Res::Def(_)          => { "Def" }
            Res::Local(_)        => { "Local" }
            Res::GenericParam(_) => { "GenericParam" }
            Res::PrimTy(_)       => { "PrimTy" }
            Res::SourceLoc(k)    => { fmt::format("SourceLoc(%s)", k.to_string()) }
            Res::TypeRelative(b, n) => {
                fmt::format("TypeRelative(%s, %d)", b.to_string(), n)
            }
            Res::Err             => { "Err" }
        };
    }
}

/// The slot a node carries: either no resolver has answered yet, or one has.
///
/// This type exists so that `Pending` is not a `Res`.  A consumer reaches an
/// answer only through `require`, which is total over `Res` - so the code that
/// would re-derive a name has no state to test for and no branch to live in.
/// The resolver writes through `answer`, which is first-write-wins, and that
/// is a correctness rule rather than an optimization: a `Res` records what the
/// file that WROTE the path can see, so a second walk standing in a different
/// module must not be able to overwrite the first answer with its own view.
type enum ResSlot {
    /// No resolver has answered for this node yet.
    Pending;
    /// A resolver has answered, including with a failure.
    Answered(Res);
}

implement enum ResSlot {
    /// Whether no resolver has answered yet.
    ///
    /// For DIAGNOSING an unstamped node at a consumer that holds it, never for
    /// deciding what to do about one: a consumer that branches on absence is
    /// the fallback this type exists to make unwriteable.  `require` remains
    /// the only way to reach an answer.
    is_pending(&this) -> boolean {
        return match (&this) {
            ResSlot::Answered(_) => { false }
            _                    => { true }
        };
    }

    /// The local binding this slot's answer names, or an invalid symbol.
    ///
    /// An unanswered node names no binding: whatever it spells was decided by
    /// nobody, so a rib keyed by identity must not match it.  That surfaces as
    /// the use staying outside the pass's state rather than as a spelling
    /// being adopted, which is the failure this key exists to prevent.
    local_sym(&this) -> SymbolID {
        return match (&this) {
            ResSlot::Answered(r) => { r.local_sym() }
            _                    => { SymbolID::invalid() }
        };
    }

    /// The type parameter this slot's answer names, or an invalid symbol.
    ///
    /// Not a door around `require`: the question is "which parameter is
    /// this", and a node no resolver answered for names none - it is a
    /// spelling the substitution tables must not match, because whatever it
    /// spells was decided by nobody.  An unanswered node that WAS meant to
    /// name a parameter is its synthesizer's defect, and it surfaces as the
    /// parameter staying unresolved rather than as a spelling being adopted.
    generic_param_sym(&this) -> SymbolID {
        return match (&this) {
            ResSlot::Answered(r) => { r.generic_param_sym() }
            _                    => { SymbolID::invalid() }
        };
    }

    /// The type parameter a type-relative answer is rooted at, as
    /// `generic_param_sym` for the parameter itself.
    relative_param_sym(&this) -> SymbolID {
        return match (&this) {
            ResSlot::Answered(r) => { r.relative_param_sym() }
            _                    => { SymbolID::invalid() }
        };
    }

    /// Record an answer.  The first one wins; later ones are dropped.
    answer(mut &this, r: Res) -> void {
        match (&this) {
            ResSlot::Answered(_) => { return; }
            _                    => { }
        }
        *this = ResSlot::Answered(r);
    }

    /// The answer this node carries.
    ///
    /// `Pending` here means no resolver claimed a node that a later stage
    /// depends on, which is an internal error and never a licence to guess.
    /// It is recorded rather than fatal: a compilation that has already
    /// emitted a diagnostic may legitimately have skipped work, so the bug is
    /// reported only if the build otherwise succeeds (see
    /// `pending_bug_count`).  The value handed back is `Err`, which stops the
    /// caller for the same reason a real failure would - there is deliberately
    /// no third outcome for a caller to route around.
    require(&this, site: string) -> Res {
        return match (&this) {
            ResSlot::Answered(r) => { r }
            _                    => {
                record_pending_bug(site);
                Res::Err
            }
        };
    }
}

/// How many canonical names a consumer could not find a declaration for, and
/// where the first one was.  Distinct from the `Pending` tally below: a
/// `Res::Def` IS an answer, so reaching one the index cannot serve means the
/// declaration never registered, not that a pass never ran.
mut g_unregistered_defs: u64 = 0;
mut g_unregistered_first_site: string = "";

/// Note that a consumer held a canonical name no declaration answers to.
public function record_unregistered_def(site: string) -> void {
    if (g_unregistered_defs == 0) { g_unregistered_first_site = site; }
    g_unregistered_defs = g_unregistered_defs + 1;
}

/// How many canonical names named no declaration.  Zero is the only value a
/// successful build may end on.
public function unregistered_def_count() -> u64 {
    return g_unregistered_defs;
}

/// Where the first unregistered name was consumed, for the report.
public function unregistered_def_first_site() -> string {
    return g_unregistered_first_site;
}

/// How many type-declaration nodes reached a consumer with no registration
/// stamped on them, and where the first one was.  Distinct from both tallies
/// above: the stamp is written by the registration that minted it, in a
/// stage with no error exit before that registration, so a missing one is
/// never the consequence of a user error and is reported whatever else the
/// build said - deferring it would hide the defect behind the cascade it
/// causes (a method that cannot find `this`, a field that is not on its own
/// type).
mut g_unstamped_decls: u64 = 0;
mut g_unstamped_first_site: string = "";

/// Note that a consumer held a type declaration no registration stamped.
public function record_unstamped_decl(site: string) -> void {
    if (g_unstamped_decls == 0) { g_unstamped_first_site = site; }
    g_unstamped_decls = g_unstamped_decls + 1;
}

/// How many type declarations reached a consumer unstamped.
public function unstamped_decl_count() -> u64 {
    return g_unstamped_decls;
}

/// Where the first unstamped declaration was consumed, for the report.
public function unstamped_decl_first_site() -> string {
    return g_unstamped_first_site;
}

/// How many `require` calls found a `Pending` slot, and where the first one
/// was.  Held here rather than on the diagnostic engine because a `Res` slot
/// is reachable from parts of the tree that cannot see a
/// `CompilationContext`; the flush that turns this into a diagnostic runs
/// where one is in hand.
mut g_pending_bugs: u64 = 0;
mut g_pending_first_site: string = "";


/// Note that a stage required an answer no resolver had written.
public function record_pending_bug(site: string) -> void {
    if (g_pending_bugs == 0) { g_pending_first_site = site; }
    g_pending_bugs = g_pending_bugs + 1;
}

/// How many times a required answer was missing.  Zero is the only value a
/// successful build may end on.
public function pending_bug_count() -> u64 {
    return g_pending_bugs;
}

/// Where the first missing answer was required, for the report.
public function pending_bug_first_site() -> string {
    return g_pending_first_site;
}
